Let $X$ be a smooth complex projective variety and fix numerical invariants (a Hilbert polynomial with respect to a fixed ample polarization, or a Chern character). Let $M = M_X(v)$ denote the Gieseker moduli space of $S$-equivalence classes of Gieseker semistable coherent sheaves on $X$ with these invariants. Recall that a semistable sheaf $E$ is called properly semistable if it is not stable; equivalently, $E$ admits a Jordan–Hölder filtration whose graded object $\mathrm{gr}(E) = \bigoplus_i E_i$ is a non-trivial direct sum of stable sheaves of the same reduced Hilbert polynomial as $E$, and the $S$-equivalence class of $E$ is represented by $\mathrm{gr}(E)$.

Assume that a notion of Iitaka dimension $\kappa(E) \in \{-\infty, 0, 1, \dots, \dim X\}$ has been defined for properly semistable sheaves on $X$ in such a way that it depends only on the $S$-equivalence class (so that it descends to a function $\kappa : M^{\mathrm{pss}} \to \{-\infty, 0, 1, \dots, \dim X\}$, where $M^{\mathrm{pss}} \subset M$ is the closed subset parametrizing $S$-equivalence classes of properly semistable sheaves).

For each $d \in \{-\infty, 0, 1, \dots, \dim X\}$, define the Iitaka stratum
$$M^{\mathrm{pss}}_d := \{[E] \in M^{\mathrm{pss}} : \kappa(E) = d\}.$$

Determine the geometric features of the resulting stratification $M^{\mathrm{pss}} = \bigsqcup_d M^{\mathrm{pss}}_d$. In particular, describe:
(i) whether each stratum $M^{\mathrm{pss}}_d$ is locally closed (and the closure relations among strata);
(ii) the dimensions of the strata, and whether they admit natural scheme or stack structures;
(iii) singularities and irreducible component structure of the strata;
(iv) how the stratification interacts with the Jordan–Hölder decomposition, i.e. with the natural maps from products of moduli spaces of stable factors to $M^{\mathrm{pss}}$.

